RationalMaps -- rational maps between projective varieties
**********************************************************

Description
===========

RationalMaps is a package for computing things related to maps between
projective varieties.

It focuses on finding where a birational map is undefined, checking whether a
map is a closed embedding, checking birationality and computing inverse maps

Mathematical background:
  * A. V. Dória, S. H. Hassanzadeh, A. Simis,    A characteristic free
    criterion of birationality, Advances in Mathematics, Volume 230, Issue 1, 1
    May 2012, Pages 390-413.
  * A. Simis,   Cremona Transformations and some Related Algebras, Journal of
    Algebra, Volume 280, Issue 1, 1 October 2004, Pages 162--179
Functionality overlap with other packages:  

*note Parametrization: (Parametrization)Top,:  While the package *note
Parametrization: (Parametrization)Top, focuses mostly on curves, it also
includes a function *note invertBirationalMap:
(Parametrization)invertBirationalMap, that has the same functionality as *note
inverseOfMap: inverseOfMap,.  On the other hand, these two functions were
implemented differently and so sometimes one function can be substantially
faster than the other.

*note Cremona: (Cremona)Top,:  The package *note Cremona: (Cremona)Top, focuses
on  fast probabilistic computations in general cases and  deterministic
computations for special kinds of maps from projective space.  More precisely,
  * *note isBirational: (Cremona)isBirational, gives a probabilistic answer to
    the question of whether a map between varieties is birational.
    Furthermore, if the source is projective space, then degreeOfRationalMap
    with MathMode=>true gives a deterministic correct answer. In some cases,
    the speed of the latter  is comparable with *note isBirationalMap:
    isBirationalMap, with AssumeDominant=>true.
  * *note inverseMap: (Cremona)inverseMap, gives a  fast computation of the
    inverse of a birational map if the source is projective space  and the map
    has maximal linear rank.   In some cases, even if the map has maximal
    linear rank, our function *note inverseOfMap: inverseOfMap, appears to be
    competitive however.  If you pass inverseMap a map not from projective
    space, then it calls a modified and improved version of *note
    invertBirationalMap: (Parametrization)invertBirationalMap, from *note
    Parametrization: (Parametrization)Top,.

