idealOfImageOfMap -- finds defining equations for the image of a rational map between varieties or schemes
**********************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        im = idealOfImageOfMap(p)
        im = idealOfImageOfMap(phi)
  * Inputs:
      * p, a *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,, corresponding to a
        rational map of projective varieties
      * phi, an instance of the type *note RationalMapping: RationalMapping,, a
        rational map between projective varieties
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * QuickRank => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value true, whether to compute rank via the package *note FastMinors:
        (FastMinors)Top,
      * Verbosity => ..., default value 0
  * Outputs:
      * im, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, defining equations for the
        image

Description
===========

Given a rational map $f : X \to Y \subset P^N$, idealOfImageOfMap returns the
defining ideal of the image of $f$ in $P^N$. The rings provided implicitly in
the inputs should be polynomial rings or quotients of polynomial rings. In
particular, idealOfImageOfMap function returns an ideal defining a subset of
the ambient projective space of the image.  In the following example we
consider the image of $P^1$ inside $P^1 \times P^1$.

+---------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = QQ[x,y,z,w];                              |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : b = ideal(x*y-z*w);                           |
|                                                   |
|o2 : Ideal of S                                    |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : R = QQ[u,v];                                  |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : a = ideal(sub(0,R));                          |
|                                                   |
|o4 : Ideal of R                                    |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : f = matrix {{u,0,v,0}};                       |
|                                                   |
|             1      4                              |
|o5 : Matrix R  <-- R                               |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : phi = rationalMapping(R/a, S/b, f)            |
|                                                   |
|                             S                     |
|o6 = Proj R - - - > Proj(---------)   {u, 0, v, 0} |
|                         x*y - z*w                 |
|                                                   |
|o6 : RationalMapping                               |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : idealOfImageOfMap(phi)                        |
|                                                   |
|o7 = ideal (w, y)                                  |
|                                                   |
|                  S                                |
|o7 : Ideal of ---------                            |
|              x*y - z*w                            |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : psi = rationalMapping(Proj(S/b), Proj(R/a), f)|
|                                                   |
|                             S                     |
|o8 = Proj R - - - > Proj(---------)   {u, 0, v, 0} |
|                         x*y - z*w                 |
|                                                   |
|o8 : RationalMapping                               |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : idealOfImageOfMap(psi)                        |
|                                                   |
|o9 = ideal (w, y)                                  |
|                                                   |
|                  S                                |
|o9 : Ideal of ---------                            |
|              x*y - z*w                            |
+---------------------------------------------------+

This function frequently just calls *note ker:
(Macaulay2Doc)kernel_lpRingMap_rp, from Macaulay2.  However, if the target of
the ring map is a polynomial ring, then it first tries to verify whether the
ring map is injective.  This is done by computing the rank of an appropriate
Jacobian matrix.

