isEmbedding -- whether a rational map of projective varieties is a closed embedding
***********************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        val = isEmbedding(f)
        val = isEmbedding(phi)
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,, the ring map corresponding
        to $f : X \to Y$
      * phi, an instance of the type *note RationalMapping: RationalMapping,, a
        rational map of projective varieties, $f : X \to Y$.
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Verbosity => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 1, if 0
        then silence the function, if 1 then generate informative output which
        can be used to adjust strategies, if > 1 then generate a detailed
        description of the execution
      * AssumeDominant => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,,
        default value false, whether to assume the provided rational map of
        projective varieties is dominant, if set to true it can speed up
        computation
      * Strategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default value
        HybridStrategy, choose the strategy to use: HybridStrategy,
        SimisStrategy, or ReesStrategy
      * HybridLimit => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 15,
        within HybridStrategy, the option HybridLimit controls how often
        SimisStrategy and ReesStrategy are used,   larger numbers means
        SimisStrategy will be executed longer
      * MinorsLimit => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value 0,
        how many submatrices of a variant of the Jacobian dual matrix to
        consider before switching to a different strategy
      * QuickRank => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value true, whether to compute rank via the package *note FastMinors:
        (FastMinors)Top,
  * Outputs:
      * val, a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, true if the map is
        an embedding, otherwise false.

Description
===========

Given a map of rings, corresponding to a rational map $f : X \to Y$,
isEmbedding determines whether $f$ map embeds $X$ as a closed subscheme into
$Y$.  The target and source must be varieties; their defining ideals must be
prime.  Consider the Veronese embedding.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/7[x,y];                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S = ZZ/7[a,b,c];                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : h = map(R, S, {x^2, x*y, y^2});                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : RingMap R <-- S                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isEmbedding(h, Verbosity=>1)                                            |
|isEmbedding: About to find the image of the map.  If you know the image,     |
|        you may want to use the AssumeDominant option if this is slow.       |
|inverseOfMapSimis: About to check rank, if this is very slow, you may want to|
|inverseOfMapSimis: rank found, we computed enough of the Groebner basis.     |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = true                                                                    |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|try turning QuickRank=>false.                                                |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

If the option Verbosity is set to 2, the function will produce very detailed
output.  Setting it to 0 will suppress output such output. Now consider the
projection from a point on the plane to the line at infinity.

+------------------------------------+
|i5 : R=QQ[x,y,z];                   |
+------------------------------------+
|i6 : S=QQ[a,b];                     |
+------------------------------------+
|i7 : h=rationalMapping(R, S, {y,z});|
+------------------------------------+
|i8 : isEmbedding(h, Verbosity=>0)   |
|                                    |
|o8 = false                          |
+------------------------------------+

That is obviously not an embedding.  It is even not an embedding when we
restrict to a quadratic curve, even though it is a regular map.

+----------------------------------+
|i9 : R=QQ[x,y,z]/(x^2+y^2-z^2);   |
+----------------------------------+
|i10 : S=QQ[a,b];                  |
+----------------------------------+
|i11 : h=map(R,S, {y,z});          |
|                                  |
|o11 : RingMap R <-- S             |
+----------------------------------+
|i12 : isRegularMap(h)             |
|                                  |
|o12 = true                        |
+----------------------------------+
|i13 : isEmbedding(h, Verbosity=>0)|
|                                  |
|o13 = false                       |
+----------------------------------+

If the option AssumeDominant is set to true, the function won't compute the
kernel of the ring map.  Otherwise it will.

The remaining options, Strategy, HybridLimit, MinorsLimit, and CheckBirational
are simply passed when isEmbedding calls inverseOfMap.  Note, this function,
isEmbedding, will only behave properly if CheckBirational is set to true.

We conclude by considering the map from $P^1$ to a cuspidal curve in $P^2$.
This is not an embedding, but if we take the strict transform in the blowup of
$P^2$, it is an embedding.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : R = ZZ/103[x,y,z];                                             |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : T = ZZ/103[u,v];                                               |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i16 : P2 = Proj R;                                                   |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i17 : P1 = Proj T;                                                   |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i18 : phi = rationalMapping(P2, P1, {u^3, u^2*v, v^3});              |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i19 : isEmbedding(phi, Verbosity=>0)                                 |
|                                                                     |
|o19 = false                                                          |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i20 : P5ring = ZZ/103[a..f];                                         |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i21 : M = matrix{{a,b,c},{d,e,f}};                                   |
|                                                                     |
|                   2           3                                     |
|o21 : Matrix P5ring  <-- P5ring                                      |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i22 : blowUpSubvar = Proj(P5ring/(minors(2, M)+ideal(b - d)));       |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i23 : tau = rationalMapping(P2, blowUpSubvar,{a, b, c}); --the blowup|
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i24 : tauInverse = tau^-1; --the inverse blowup                      |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i25 : isEmbedding(tauInverse*phi, Verbosity => 0)                    |
|                                                                     |
|o25 = true                                                           |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note HybridStrategy: HybridStrategy, -- A strategy for determining whether
    a map is birational and computing its inverse
  * *note SimisStrategy: SimisStrategy, -- a strategy for determining whether a
    map is birational and computing its inverse
  * *note ReesStrategy: ReesStrategy, -- a strategy for determining whether a
    map is birational and computing its inverse

Ways to use isEmbedding:
========================

  * "isEmbedding(RationalMapping)"
  * "isEmbedding(RingMap)"

