jacobianDualMatrix -- computes the Jacobian dual matrix
*******************************************************

  * Usage: 
        M = jacobianDualMatrix(p)
        M = jacobianDualMatrix(phi)
  * Inputs:
      * phi, an instance of the type *note RationalMapping: RationalMapping,, a
        rational map between projective varieties
      * p, a *note ring map: (Macaulay2Doc)RingMap,, ring map corresponding to
        a rational map between projective varieties
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Strategy => a *note symbol: (Macaulay2Doc)Symbol,, default value
        ReesStrategy, choose the strategy to use: ReesStrategy or
        SaturationStrategy
      * AssumeDominant => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,,
        default value false, whether to assume the provided rational map of
        projective varieties is dominant, if set to true it can speed up
        computation
      * QuickRank => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value true, whether to compute rank via the package *note FastMinors:
        (FastMinors)Top,
  * Outputs:
      * M, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, a matrix $M$ over the
        coordinate ring of the image, the kernel of $M$ describes the syzygies
        of the inverse map, if it exists.

Description
===========

The Jacobian dual matrix is a matrix whose kernel describes the syzygies of the
matrix corresponding to the inverse map. For more information, see

  * Doria, A. V.; Hassanzadeh, S. H.; Simis, A. A characteristic-free criterion
    of birationality. Adv. Math. 230 (2012), no. 1, 390--413.

This is mostly an internal function. It is used when checking whether a map is
birational and when computing the inverse map.  If the AssumeDominant option is
set to true, it assumes that the kernel of the associated ring map is zero
(default value is false).  Valid values for the Strategy option are
ReesStrategy and SaturationStrategy.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R=QQ[x,y];                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : S=QQ[a,b,c,d];                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : Pi = map(R, S, {x^3, x^2*y, x*y^2, y^3});                               |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : RingMap R <-- S                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : jacobianDualMatrix(Pi, Strategy=>SaturationStrategy)                    |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = | -d -c -b 0 0 0 |                                                      |
|     | c  b  a  0 0 0 |                                                      |
|                                                                             |
|            /               S               \2     /               S         |
|o4 : Matrix |-------------------------------|  <-- |-------------------------|
|            |  2                    2       |      |  2                    2 |
|            \(c  - b*d, b*c - a*d, b  - a*c)/      \(c  - b*d, b*c - a*d, b  |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|      \6                                                                     |
|------|                                                                      |
|      |                                                                      |
|- a*c)/                                                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

See also
========

  * *note HybridStrategy: HybridStrategy, -- A strategy for determining whether
    a map is birational and computing its inverse
  * *note SimisStrategy: SimisStrategy, -- a strategy for determining whether a
    map is birational and computing its inverse
  * *note ReesStrategy: ReesStrategy, -- a strategy for determining whether a
    map is birational and computing its inverse

Ways to use jacobianDualMatrix:
===============================

  * "jacobianDualMatrix(RationalMapping)"
  * "jacobianDualMatrix(RingMap)"

